select 'bbbb0004-0000-4000-8000-00000000002c', v.pos, v.title, v.kind, v.body::jsonb, v.notes, v.mins
from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Locard''s exchange principle is the idea that...","options":["every contact leaves a trace","every criminal eventually confesses","fingerprints are always unique","DNA never degrades"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When two things touch, material moves both ways - the founding idea of trace evidence, covered in ''Locard''s exchange principle''."},{"prompt":"A DNA profile used in casework is built mainly from...","options":["the person''s whole genome sequence","short tandem repeats (STRs) at set locations","blood type alone","the shape of the chromosomes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Standard profiling counts repeats at a fixed panel of STR loci - see ''DNA profiling''."},{"prompt":"If a matching profile is found in roughly 1 person in 1,000,000, the random match probability is...","options":["1 in 1,000,000","1,000,000 to 1","certainty of guilt","0"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Random match probability is the chance an unrelated person shares the profile by chance - here 1 in a million."},{"prompt":"The ''prosecutor''s fallacy'' confuses...","options":["two different suspects","the chance of the evidence given innocence with the chance of innocence given the evidence","DNA with fingerprints","a photograph with a witness"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Those two conditional probabilities are not the same - the heart of ''The statistics of evidence''."},{"prompt":"Fingerprint ridge patterns are broadly classified as...","options":["lines, dots, and gaps","loops, whorls, and arches","primary, secondary, tertiary","hot, warm, and cold"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The three top-level pattern families are loops, whorls, and arches - see ''Fingerprints''."},{"prompt":"A likelihood ratio compares the probability of the evidence under...","options":["one hypothesis only","two competing hypotheses","the judge''s opinion","the cost of the trial"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A likelihood ratio divides P(evidence | prosecution view) by P(evidence | defence view)."},{"prompt":"Bloodstain pattern analysis studies mainly the...","options":["chemical make-up of blood","size, shape, and distribution of stains","age of the victim","colour of clothing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Patterns and shapes let an analyst reason about how drops travelled and landed - see ''Bloodstain pattern analysis''."},{"prompt":"In digital forensics, a ''hash value'' is used to...","options":["speed up a computer","check that a copy of data is identical to the original","delete evidence","encrypt a suspect''s phone"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A hash is a fixed-length fingerprint of a file; if two hashes match, the copies are treated as identical - see ''Digital forensics''."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'What forensic science is: from scene to court', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe what forensic science does, name the stages that carry material from a scene to a courtroom, and say where the scientist''s job stops and the court''s job begins. Warm-up: if a scientist reports that two fibres ''could have come from the same jumper,'' have they proved who was present?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Forensic science applies ordinary science - chemistry, biology, physics, statistics - to questions that matter in a legal case. The work follows a chain: recognise and record what is at a scene, recover and package it without contaminating it, analyse it in a laboratory, interpret what the results mean, and finally report in a form a court can weigh. Two ideas run through the whole chain. The first is continuity, often called chain of custody: every item must be traceable from collection to court so no one can claim it was swapped or altered. The second is that the scientist evaluates evidence but does not decide guilt; they report how strongly the findings support one account over another, and the court decides. A result is only as trustworthy as the weakest link in that chain."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A recovered glass fragment matches a broken window in composition. A careful report does not say ''the suspect broke the window.'' It says the fragment shares measurable properties with the window and gives how common those properties are, so the court can judge weight. Another example: a laboratory finds a substance is a controlled drug. That is a factual chemical result. Whether the person knew they possessed it is a legal question, not a chemical one. Notice the pattern in both: the scientist supplies a measured, bounded statement, and leaves the leap to a verdict to the court."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Put these in order: analyse, recover, report, recognise, interpret. 2) What is ''chain of custody'' protecting against? 3) True or false: a forensic scientist should state in the report who is guilty. 4) Give one reason a sample might be worthless even if the analysis is perfect. 5) Which stage is where contamination is easiest to introduce? Answers: 1) recognise, recover, analyse, interpret, report. 2) The claim that an item was swapped, lost, or altered between scene and court. 3) False - that is the court''s decision. 4) It was collected or stored so poorly that it was contaminated or its origin cannot be shown. 5) Recovery and packaging at the scene, before controlled lab conditions apply."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating a laboratory ''match'' as a statement of guilt rather than of shared properties. Mistake: ignoring continuity, so a perfect analysis rests on a sample of unknown origin. Exit: (a) Who decides guilt, the scientist or the court? (b) Name the two ideas that run through the whole scene-to-court chain. Answers: the court; continuity (chain of custody) and evaluating evidence rather than deciding guilt."}]}'::jsonb, 'Keep three ideas separate: a ''trace'' is physical material, ''evidence'' is a trace interpreted in a case, and ''proof'' is a legal conclusion for the court to reach - not the scientist.', 13),
  (3, 'Locard''s exchange principle and trace evidence', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"State Locard''s exchange principle, explain what trace evidence is, and reason about why a trace might be present, absent, or misleading. Warm-up: name two everyday materials that transfer easily when two people brush past each other."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Locard''s exchange principle says that when two surfaces come into contact, material passes in both directions: you leave something and you take something away. Trace evidence is the small stuff this produces - fibres, hairs, paint flakes, glass fragments, soil, gunshot residue. Trace evidence is valued because it is easy to transfer and hard to notice, so a person may carry it without knowing. But three cautions matter. Transfer can be direct (surface to surface) or secondary (via an intermediate, such as a shared car seat), which complicates who touched what. Persistence varies: a fibre may fall off in minutes while embedded glass lasts for days. And background matters: a common blue cotton fibre is far less informative than a rare dyed one, because many people carry the common type by chance."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Suppose a rare red acrylic fibre is found on a jacket and matches a specific car''s seat covers. Because the fibre type is uncommon, its presence carries more weight than a plain white cotton fibre would. Now suppose the same fibre is found on a friend who only ever sat in that car - that is secondary transfer, and it shows contact with the car, not with any person. A contrast case: no fibres at all are recovered. That does not show the person was never in the car; the fibres may simply have shed and been lost. In each case the question is not ''present or absent?'' alone, but ''how likely is this result if the contact happened, versus if it did not?''"},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) State Locard''s principle in one sentence. 2) Which is more informative, a common fibre or a rare one, and why? 3) Define secondary transfer. 4) A search finds no gunshot residue on a suspect. Does that prove they did not fire a gun? 5) Give one factor that affects how long a trace persists. Answers: 1) When two things make contact, material transfers both ways. 2) A rare fibre, because fewer people carry it by chance, so a match is less easily coincidental. 3) Transfer of material through an intermediate surface rather than by direct contact. 4) No - residue can be absent, washed off, or lost, so absence is not proof. 5) The type of material and surface, movement, time, and washing all affect persistence."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: reading a found trace as direct contact when secondary transfer could explain it. Mistake: treating absence of a trace as proof that nothing happened. Exit: (a) A trace is more informative when it is common or when it is rare? (b) True or false: not finding a fibre proves no contact. Answers: rare; false."}]}'::jsonb, '''Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence'' - failing to find a trace does not prove no contact happened; traces can be tiny, lost, or never transferred.', 13),
  (4, 'Fingerprints: formation, classification, and matching', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain how friction-ridge skin forms fingerprints, name the main pattern classes and the features used to compare prints, and describe why a comparison is a judgement, not a mechanical certainty. Warm-up: are the fingerprints of identical twins identical?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Fingerprints come from friction ridges on the skin of the fingers, formed before birth as skin layers grow and buckle. The broad pattern is shaped partly by genetics, but the fine detail is shaped by random events in the womb, which is why even identical twins differ. Patterns are classified at the top level as loops, whorls, and arches. Comparison, though, does not rely on the overall pattern; it relies on minutiae - small features such as where a ridge ends (a ridge ending) or splits in two (a bifurcation). An examiner compares the type, position, and relationship of many minutiae between a mark from a scene and a known print. Because scene marks are often smudged or partial, the examiner is judging whether the agreement is enough to conclude common origin, and quality can be poor. This is expert judgement, and studies show it can carry error, so good practice uses verification by a second examiner."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Imagine a scene mark shows a ridge ending, then two bifurcations spaced a set number of ridges apart, in a loop. A candidate print shows the same three features in the same arrangement, plus more that agree and none that clearly disagree. The examiner may conclude common origin. Now imagine the mark is a small smudge with only two usable features: that may be too little to decide either way, and the honest report is ''inconclusive.'' A contrast: if the candidate print has a clear feature exactly where the mark shows a different, incompatible feature, that is an exclusion - they did not come from the same finger. The reasoning is always about agreement and disagreement of detail, weighed against how much usable detail exists."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name the three top-level pattern classes. 2) What are minutiae? 3) Why do identical twins have different fingerprints? 4) Give the term for an invisible print that must be developed. 5) What should happen when a mark has too few usable features to decide? Answers: 1) Loops, whorls, and arches. 2) Small ridge features such as ridge endings and bifurcations used for comparison. 3) Fine ridge detail is set by random events during development, not by genes alone. 4) A latent print. 5) The result should be reported as inconclusive, not forced to a match."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming a fingerprint comparison is a mechanical certainty rather than an expert judgement that can err. Mistake: matching on overall pattern class instead of the detailed minutiae. Exit: (a) Which features drive a fingerprint comparison, the broad pattern or the minutiae? (b) What guards against a single examiner''s error? Answers: the minutiae; independent verification by a second examiner."}]}'::jsonb, 'A ''latent'' print is invisible and must be developed; a ''patent'' print is already visible; a ''plastic'' print is pressed into a soft surface. The word latent means hidden, not fake.', 14),
  (5, 'DNA profiling: STRs, profiles, and match probability', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain what a DNA profile is, how short tandem repeats make one, and how a random match probability is estimated and read correctly. Warm-up: if two profiles agree at every tested location, does that prove they came from the same person?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A DNA profile does not read a person''s whole genome. It looks at a fixed panel of locations, or loci, where a short sequence repeats a variable number of times - these are short tandem repeats, or STRs. At each locus a person inherits one count from each parent, so they have a pair of numbers. The profile is just the list of pairs across all the tested loci. Because the counts vary widely between people and the loci are chosen to be roughly independent, the combined profile is rare. To estimate how rare, you find how common each locus''s pair is in a population and multiply those frequencies together (the product rule for independent events). The result is the random match probability: the chance that an unrelated person picked at random would share this profile. A tiny random match probability means a coincidental match is very unlikely, but it is a statement about coincidence, not a statement of guilt."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Suppose at one locus the person''s pair occurs in about 1 in 10 people, at a second locus about 1 in 12, and at a third about 1 in 8. Treating the loci as independent, the chance of sharing all three by chance is (1/10) x (1/12) x (1/8) = 1 in 960. Real profiles use far more loci, so the numbers get very small - for example, combining enough loci might give a random match probability of about 1 in a billion. Read that carefully: it means roughly one unrelated person in a billion would match by chance, not that there is a one-in-a-billion chance the suspect is innocent. A second caution: close relatives share more DNA, so the ''unrelated person'' figure understates how likely a sibling would match - that must be handled separately."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What does STR stand for? 2) A profile records how many numbers at each locus, and why? 3) At two independent loci with frequencies 1 in 20 and 1 in 5, what is the combined random match probability? 4) Does a random match probability of 1 in a million mean a 1-in-a-million chance of innocence? 5) Why do relatives complicate the calculation? Answers: 1) Short tandem repeat. 2) Two - one repeat count inherited from each parent. 3) (1/20) x (1/5) = 1 in 100. 4) No - it is the chance an unrelated person matches by chance, a different quantity. 5) Relatives share more DNA, so they match far more often than an unrelated person, and the standard figure assumes no relation."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: reading the random match probability as the probability of innocence. Mistake: multiplying frequencies when loci are not independent or when a relative is a realistic alternative. Exit: (a) What quantity do you multiply across loci to get the random match probability? (b) Does a match prove identity on its own? Answers: the per-locus frequencies of the observed pairs; no - it makes coincidence unlikely but must be weighed with the rest of the case."}]}'::jsonb, '''Random match probability'' is the chance an unrelated person shares the profile by chance. It is not the chance the suspect is innocent - keep those two ideas apart (see ''The statistics of evidence'').', 15),
  (6, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Locard''s exchange principle predicts that after contact between two surfaces...","options":["nothing measurable changes","material transfers in both directions","only fingerprints remain","the surfaces become identical"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Contact moves material both ways, which is why trace evidence exists."},{"prompt":"A rare fibre matching a suspect''s car is generally more informative than a common one because...","options":["rare fibres are heavier","fewer people carry it by chance, so a match is less coincidental","common fibres cannot transfer","rare fibres never shed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The weight of a match depends on how often it could arise by coincidence."},{"prompt":"Fingerprint comparison relies mainly on...","options":["the overall loop-or-whorl class","the colour of the ink","minutiae such as ridge endings and bifurcations","the size of the finger"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Examiners compare the detailed minutiae, not just the broad pattern class."},{"prompt":"A DNA profile is built from...","options":["the entire genome sequence","repeat counts at a fixed panel of STR loci","blood type only","the number of chromosomes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Profiling records STR repeat counts at chosen loci - see ''DNA profiling''."},{"prompt":"At two independent loci with frequencies 1 in 10 and 1 in 4, the combined random match probability is...","options":["1 in 14","1 in 40","1 in 4","1 in 2.5"],"answer":1,"explanation":"(1/10) x (1/4) = 1 in 40 by the product rule for independent events."},{"prompt":"Finding no gunshot residue on a suspect...","options":["proves they never fired a gun","is meaningless in every case","does not prove they never fired, since residue can be lost","always means the test failed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Absence of a trace is not proof of absence of the event."}]}'::jsonb, null, 9),
  (7, 'The chemistry of evidence: drugs and toxicology basics', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain the difference between presumptive and confirmatory chemical tests, describe at a high level how substances are separated and identified, and say what toxicology can and cannot tell you about timing and dose. Warm-up: if a quick colour test turns the expected colour, has the substance been identified?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Analytical chemistry identifies unknown substances in two tiers. A presumptive test is fast and cheap - often a colour change - and narrows the possibilities, but several substances can give the same reaction, so it can only suggest. A confirmatory test is more powerful and specific. A common approach separates a mixture into its components and then identifies each one by a property unique to its molecules, such as its mass pattern; separation followed by identification is the workhorse of the field. Toxicology applies the same tools to body samples such as blood or urine to detect drugs, alcohol, or poisons. But interpretation is hard: a concentration measured now must be related back to some earlier time, and the body processes substances at rates that vary between people, so back-calculating a past level or a dose is uncertain. Tolerance also means the same concentration can affect two people very differently."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A colour spot test on a powder turns the shade associated with a class of drugs. That is presumptive: it is consistent with that class but does not identify the exact substance, because look-alike compounds can react the same way. A confirmatory run then separates the powder''s components and identifies one by its characteristic molecular signature, allowing a firm statement of what it is. In toxicology, suppose a blood sample taken two hours after an incident shows a certain alcohol concentration. Estimating the concentration at the earlier time requires assuming an elimination rate; because that rate varies between people, the back-calculation should be reported as a range, not a single exact number. The lesson repeats: a fast test narrows, a specific test identifies, and timing turns a measurement into an estimate with uncertainty."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What can a presumptive test do, and what can it not do? 2) Name the two-step strategy used to identify components of a mixture. 3) Why is back-calculating a past drug or alcohol level uncertain? 4) True or false: a colour change is enough to identify a specific drug. 5) Why can the same blood concentration affect two people differently? Answers: 1) It narrows the possibilities but cannot identify the exact substance. 2) Separate the mixture into components, then identify each by a specific property such as its mass pattern. 3) The body clears substances at rates that vary between people, so the earlier value is an estimate. 4) False - that is only presumptive. 5) Differences in tolerance, body size, and metabolism."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: reporting a presumptive colour test as a positive identification. Mistake: giving a single exact figure for a back-calculated past concentration instead of a range. Exit: (a) Which test type actually identifies a substance, presumptive or confirmatory? (b) Why should a back-calculated blood level be a range? Answers: confirmatory; because elimination rates vary between people, so the earlier value is uncertain."}]}'::jsonb, 'A ''presumptive'' test suggests what a substance might be; a ''confirmatory'' test identifies it with high certainty. Never report a presumptive result as an identification.', 14),
  (8, 'Comparing fibres, glass, and soil', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain how fibres, glass, and soil are compared, distinguish class evidence from individualising evidence, and reason about how common a matching property is. Warm-up: does matching the colour and type of two fibres prove they came from the very same garment?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Much physical evidence is comparison evidence: an examiner measures properties of a questioned sample and a known sample and asks whether they could share a source. Fibres are compared by colour, dye chemistry, diameter, and material type. Glass is compared by properties such as how much it bends light (refractive index) and its elemental make-up. Soil is compared by colour, mineral content, and particle sizes. The key distinction is between class evidence, which places a sample in a group shared by many items (for example, a common fibre type), and individualising evidence, which can point to a single source (a classic example being a fingerprint or a full DNA profile). Most fibre, glass, and soil comparisons yield class evidence. That is still useful, but its weight depends entirely on how common the matching class is: matching a rare property is strong, matching a widespread one is weak. A physical fit, where two broken edges rejoin like puzzle pieces, is a rare case that can approach individualisation."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Two glass fragments share a refractive index that occurs in perhaps 1 in 100 of the general glass population. A match is meaningful but not conclusive: about one fragment in a hundred would match by chance, so the court weighs it alongside everything else. Contrast a soil sample rich in an unusual mineral found only near one location - its rarity makes a match far more telling. Now a strong case: a broken headlight fragment from a scene physically fits the jagged remaining edge in a suspect vehicle''s headlight, aligning ridge for ridge. That physical fit is close to individualising, because two random breaks matching so exactly is extraordinarily unlikely. Across all three, the reasoning is the same: how often would this degree of agreement happen by chance?"},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Define class evidence. 2) Give one property used to compare glass. 3) Why is matching a rare soil mineral stronger than matching a common one? 4) What is a physical fit, and why is it powerful? 5) True or false: matching two common fibres proves a shared garment. Answers: 1) Evidence placing a sample in a group shared by many items, not a single source. 2) Refractive index (or elemental composition). 3) A rare property is far less likely to match by chance, so the coincidence explanation is weaker. 4) When two broken edges rejoin exactly; it is powerful because two random breaks matching so precisely is extremely unlikely. 5) False - it is class evidence consistent with, but not proof of, a shared source."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: reporting class evidence as if it identified a single unique source. Mistake: ignoring how common the matching property is when stating weight. Exit: (a) Which is stronger evidence of a single source, a common fibre match or a physical fit? (b) What determines the weight of a class match? Answers: a physical fit; how common (or rare) the matching class is in the relevant population."}]}'::jsonb, 'In comparison evidence, prefer ''consistent with the same source'' over ''from the same source.'' Most such evidence shows class membership, not unique identity.', 13),
  (9, 'Bloodstain pattern analysis: the basics', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain what bloodstain pattern analysis studies, describe how stain shape relates to how a drop travelled, and state the main uncertainties and limits of the method. Warm-up: would a drop that hits a surface straight down leave a rounder or a more stretched stain than one that hits at a shallow angle?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Bloodstain pattern analysis studies the size, shape, and distribution of stains to reason about the events that produced them - not whose blood it is, which is a separate DNA question. A drop falling straight onto a surface tends to leave a fairly round stain; a drop striking at a shallow angle stretches into an elongated shape whose length and width relate to that angle. Many drops together form patterns: a fine mist suggests a high-energy event, while large drops suggest passive dripping. By combining the directions and shapes of many stains, an analyst can sometimes estimate a region where the blood originated. But the method has real limits. Surfaces matter - a rough or absorbent surface distorts stain shape. Later events can overlay or wipe patterns. And research has shown that conclusions can vary between analysts, so the discipline is treated as interpretive, with uncertainty stated rather than hidden."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A nearly circular stain on smooth flooring suggests a drop that fell roughly straight down. An elongated stain with a narrow tail points in the direction the drop was travelling, so a set of such stains can indicate a common direction of travel. A region of very fine droplets suggests a forceful event that broke blood into a mist, whereas a few large round drops suggest slow passive dripping. Combining the directions of several elongated stains, an analyst might estimate an area of origin - but if the flooring is textured, the shapes distort and the estimate widens. The recurring theme: shape and distribution constrain the possibilities, and the honest output is a range of consistent scenarios, not a single certain reconstruction."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What does bloodstain pattern analysis reason about, and what does it not tell you? 2) What shape does a drop hitting straight down tend to leave? 3) What might a fine mist of tiny droplets suggest? 4) Name one factor that distorts stain shape. 5) Why is a single certain reconstruction usually the wrong output? Answers: 1) How the stains were produced; it does not tell you whose blood it is (that is DNA). 2) A fairly round stain. 3) A high-energy event that broke the blood into a mist. 4) A rough or absorbent surface (also later disturbance of the pattern). 5) Because shape and surface introduce uncertainty, so several scenarios can be consistent - the output should be a bounded range."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: using stain patterns to claim whose blood it is - that requires DNA. Mistake: giving one exact reconstruction when the surface and overlapping events allow a range. Exit: (a) A round stain suggests a steep or shallow impact angle? (b) Is bloodstain analysis a source of certainty or an interpretive method with stated uncertainty? Answers: a steep (near-vertical) impact; an interpretive method whose uncertainty should be stated."}]}'::jsonb, 'Bloodstain pattern analysis reasons about how stains were produced, not about whose blood it is - ''whose'' is a DNA question handled separately.', 13),
  (10, 'Checkpoint 2', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"A presumptive colour test on an unknown powder can...","options":["identify the exact substance","narrow the possibilities but not identify it alone","measure the exact dose taken","replace a confirmatory test"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Presumptive tests suggest; confirmatory tests identify - see ''The chemistry of evidence''."},{"prompt":"Most fibre, glass, and soil comparisons produce...","options":["individualising evidence pointing to one source","class evidence shared by many items","proof of guilt","no useful information"],"answer":1,"explanation":"They usually establish class membership, whose weight depends on how common the class is."},{"prompt":"A glass refractive index shared by about 1 in 100 fragments means a match is...","options":["conclusive proof of a single source","meaningful but not conclusive on its own","completely worthless","individualising"],"answer":1,"explanation":"About one fragment in a hundred matches by chance, so it is weighed with the rest of the case."},{"prompt":"A drop of blood striking a surface at a shallow angle tends to leave a stain that is...","options":["perfectly round","elongated","invisible","square"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Shallow impact stretches the stain; steep impact leaves a rounder one."},{"prompt":"Back-calculating a past blood alcohol level is uncertain mainly because...","options":["blood cannot be tested","elimination rates vary between people","the colour changes over time","labs are unreliable"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Individual differences in how fast the body clears alcohol make the earlier value a range."},{"prompt":"A physical fit, where two broken edges rejoin exactly, is notable because it...","options":["is common and weak","can approach individualisation","identifies whose blood it is","requires no examiner judgement"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Two random breaks matching so precisely is extremely unlikely, approaching a single-source conclusion."}]}'::jsonb, null, 9),
  (11, 'Digital forensics: an overview', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe what digital forensics recovers, explain why hashing and write-blocking protect digital evidence, and reason about the limits of metadata and attribution. Warm-up: if a file was found on a laptop, does that prove the laptop''s owner created it?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Digital forensics recovers and interprets data from computers, phones, and networks - files, messages, location records, and logs. Two practices protect the integrity of that data. First, examiners work on a copy, not the original, and use a write-blocker so the act of examining cannot change the source. Second, they compute a hash value: a fixed-length fingerprint of the data such that any change, however small, produces a different hash. Matching hashes show the copy is identical to the original, preserving continuity. Interpretation, though, is where care is needed. Metadata such as timestamps can be edited, and clocks can be wrong, so a time should be checked against other records. Attribution - deciding who was at the keyboard - is especially hard: an account, a device, and a person are three different things. A shared computer, a borrowed phone, or malicious software can all break the assumption that the device owner performed an action."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"An examiner images a phone, then hashes both the original and the copy; the hashes match, so any later analysis is done on a verified duplicate. A message is recovered with a timestamp - but before relying on it, the examiner checks whether the device clock was accurate and whether network records agree, because the timestamp alone is editable. A stronger attribution case combines sources: a login from the account, a message typed in a personal style, and a linked location record all pointing the same way. A weaker case rests on a single fact: a file merely present on a shared laptop shows the file was there, not who put it there. The pattern is that digital facts are strongest when independent records corroborate, and weakest when one editable detail is asked to carry the whole claim."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Why do examiners work on a copy with a write-blocker? 2) What does a matching hash value tell you? 3) Why should a file''s timestamp be checked against other records? 4) Name three things that attribution must not treat as the same. 5) Is a file''s mere presence on a shared computer strong evidence of who created it? Answers: 1) So examining the data cannot alter the original source. 2) That the copy is identical to the original, preserving continuity. 3) Because timestamps and clocks can be wrong or edited, so they need corroboration. 4) An account, a device, and a person. 5) No - it shows the file was present, not who put it there."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating an editable timestamp as an unquestionable fact. Mistake: equating a device or account with a specific person. Exit: (a) What does a write-blocker prevent? (b) Why is attribution hard? Answers: it prevents the examination from altering the original data; because accounts, devices, and people are distinct, and sharing, borrowing, or malware can separate them."}]}'::jsonb, '''Metadata'' is data about data - such as when a file was created or last changed. It can be highly informative but is also editable, so treat it as a claim to be checked, not a fact.', 13),
  (12, 'The statistics of evidence: likelihood ratios and the prosecutor''s fallacy', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Define a likelihood ratio, use it to express the weight of evidence, and identify and avoid the prosecutor''s fallacy. Warm-up: ''the chance of this match if the suspect is innocent'' and ''the chance the suspect is innocent given the match'' - are these the same number?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The modern way to state the weight of evidence is a likelihood ratio: the probability of the evidence if the prosecution''s account is true, divided by the probability of the same evidence if the defence''s account is true. A likelihood ratio of 1 means the evidence does not favour either side; a ratio much greater than 1 supports the prosecution account; a ratio much less than 1 supports the defence. Crucially, a likelihood ratio speaks about the evidence, not about guilt. The prosecutor''s fallacy is the error of treating ''the probability of the evidence given innocence'' as if it were ''the probability of innocence given the evidence.'' They are different, and confusing them can make weak evidence sound like near-certainty. The correct way to update belief combines the likelihood ratio with the prior odds from everything else in the case - the evidence multiplies the odds, it does not set them by itself."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Suppose a profile''s random match probability is 1 in a million. If the suspect is the source, the match is essentially certain, so P(evidence | source) is about 1; if the suspect is not the source, P(evidence | not source) is about 1 in a million. The likelihood ratio is roughly 1 divided by (1 in a million) = about a million: the match is a million times more probable if the suspect is the source than if not. That is the correct statement. The fallacy would be to announce ''there is only a one-in-a-million chance the suspect is innocent'' - that swaps the conditionals and ignores the prior odds. To see why the prior matters, imagine the profile could match any of, say, several people in a large database searched with no other link; then even a tiny random match probability can throw up a coincidental hit, and the match alone is far from proof. The evidence updates the odds; it does not replace the rest of the case."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Write the likelihood ratio as a ratio of two probabilities. 2) What does a likelihood ratio of 1 mean? 3) A match is essentially certain if guilty and has probability 1 in 50,000 if innocent; what is the likelihood ratio? 4) State the prosecutor''s fallacy in one sentence. 5) Why can a database search of many profiles turn up a coincidental match? Answers: 1) P(evidence | prosecution account) divided by P(evidence | defence account). 2) The evidence favours neither side. 3) About 1 / (1/50,000) = 50,000. 4) It treats P(evidence | innocent) as if it were P(innocent | evidence), which are different quantities. 5) Because searching many people gives many chances for a rare coincidence to occur, so even a small per-person probability can produce a hit."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: reading a random match probability as the probability of innocence. Mistake: quoting a likelihood ratio while ignoring the prior odds from the rest of the case. Exit: (a) A likelihood ratio compares the evidence under how many hypotheses? (b) What error swaps P(evidence | innocent) with P(innocent | evidence)? Answers: two; the prosecutor''s fallacy."}]}'::jsonb, 'Keep two conditional probabilities apart: P(evidence | innocent) is not P(innocent | evidence). Swapping them is the prosecutor''s fallacy.', 15),
  (13, 'Presenting evidence and avoiding bias', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe how forensic conclusions should be communicated, name common sources of cognitive bias, and explain the safeguards that reduce them. Warm-up: could knowing that a suspect confessed change how an examiner reads an ambiguous fingerprint, even without meaning to?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A forensic conclusion is only useful if it is communicated in a way the court can weigh without being misled. Good practice states what was compared, how strongly the findings support one account over another (increasingly as a likelihood ratio), and the limits and assumptions behind the result. It avoids overclaiming words like ''certain'' or ''unique'' when the science supports weight rather than proof. The other half of the job is guarding against bias. Cognitive bias is an unintended pull on judgement from information that should be irrelevant to the analysis - knowing that a suspect confessed, or that a colleague already ''sees a match,'' can nudge an examiner toward that conclusion. The fixes are procedural: context management, so an analyst sees only the information they need; blind verification by a second examiner who does not know the first result; standardised methods; and honest reporting of uncertainty and error rates. The aim is a conclusion that would come out the same regardless of who the suspect is."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A report that says ''the marks are consistent, and this degree of agreement is far more probable if they share a source than if they do not'' communicates weight honestly. A report that says ''these prints are a unique and certain match'' overclaims and invites the court to skip its own weighing. On bias: if an examiner is told ''the suspect already confessed, just confirm the print,'' that irrelevant context can tilt an ambiguous call - the safeguard is to withhold it and have an independent examiner verify blind. A contrast that shows the safeguard working: two examiners, each unaware of the other''s conclusion and of the case background, independently reach the same call from the physical detail alone. Throughout, the goal is the same - conclusions driven by the evidence, insulated from expectation."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name two things a good forensic report should include besides the conclusion. 2) Give an example of information that could bias an examiner but should be irrelevant to the analysis. 3) What is blind verification? 4) Why is ''unique and certain match'' usually an overclaim? 5) What is the aim of context management? Answers: 1) How strongly the findings favour one account over another, and the limits and assumptions behind the result. 2) That the suspect confessed, or that a colleague already believes it is a match. 3) A second examiner reaching a conclusion without knowing the first examiner''s result or the case background. 4) Because most comparison evidence supports weight, not proof of a single source. 5) To let the analyst see only the information needed for the analysis, keeping irrelevant, biasing details out."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: overclaiming certainty when the science supports weight. Mistake: assuming good intentions alone prevent bias, when the fix is procedure. Exit: (a) Should conclusions depend on who the suspect is? (b) Name one procedural safeguard against cognitive bias. Answers: no - they should follow the evidence; blind verification (or context management, standardised methods, honest reporting of uncertainty)."}]}'::jsonb, '''Cognitive bias'' here means an unintended pull on judgement from irrelevant information, not dishonesty. The fix is procedure, not just good intentions.', 14),
  (14, 'Checkpoint 3', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"A matching hash value between an original and a copy of digital data shows that...","options":["the owner created the data","the copy is identical to the original","the data is encrypted","the timestamp is correct"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A hash is a fingerprint of the data; matching hashes mean the copies are identical."},{"prompt":"A likelihood ratio of 1 means the evidence...","options":["proves guilt","proves innocence","favours neither account","is inadmissible"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At 1, the evidence is equally probable under both accounts, so it favours neither."},{"prompt":"The prosecutor''s fallacy confuses...","options":["two suspects","P(evidence | innocent) with P(innocent | evidence)","glass with fibres","a copy with an original"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Those two conditional probabilities are different; swapping them overstates the evidence."},{"prompt":"If a match is essentially certain when guilty and has probability 1 in 100,000 when innocent, the likelihood ratio is about...","options":["1","100,000","1 in 100,000","0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"1 divided by (1/100,000) = 100,000 in favour of the prosecution account."},{"prompt":"Blind verification reduces bias because the second examiner...","options":["knows the first result and confirms it","does not know the first examiner''s conclusion or the case background","works faster","only checks the paperwork"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Withholding the earlier result and context keeps expectation from tilting the judgement."},{"prompt":"Attribution in digital forensics is hard because...","options":["hashes are unreliable","an account, a device, and a person are three different things","files cannot be copied","timestamps are always right"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sharing, borrowing, or malware can separate the person from the device or account."}]}'::jsonb, null, 9),
  (15, 'Capstone: reasoning about a mixed evidence set', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Pull the course together by reasoning through a made-up mixed evidence set: read each item as weight not proof, watch for the prosecutor''s fallacy, and combine independent items sensibly. Warm-up: if two separate, independent findings each moderately support the same account, is the combined support stronger or weaker than either alone?"},{"heading":"The scenario (illustrative only)","text":"Consider a fictional, teaching-only case. Three findings are reported. (1) A DNA profile from a sample matches a suspect, with a random match probability of about 1 in a million. (2) A fibre on the suspect''s coat is the same common type as one at the scene; that fibre type occurs in roughly 1 in 5 coats. (3) A device timestamp appears to place the suspect nearby, but the device clock was not verified. The task is not to decide guilt - that is the court''s job - but to reason about how much each item weighs and how they combine."},{"heading":"Worked reasoning","text":"Item 1: the correct statement is a likelihood ratio of about a million - the match is roughly a million times more probable if the suspect is the source than if an unrelated person is. It is not a one-in-a-million chance of innocence; saying so would be the prosecutor''s fallacy, and it also ignores relatives and any database-search effect. Item 2: a fibre type in 1 in 5 coats is weak class evidence; roughly one coat in five would match by chance, giving a likelihood ratio of only about 5, so it nudges rather than settles. Item 3: an unverified timestamp is a claim, not a fact - it should be checked against independent records before it carries any weight, and until then it is close to neutral. Combining: if the DNA and the fibre are genuinely independent, their likelihood ratios can be multiplied, about 1,000,000 times 5 = 5,000,000, so together they support the account more than either alone - but only because each was first stated honestly as weight, and only if independence really holds. The timestamp should be set aside until corroborated."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Restate item 1''s DNA result as a likelihood ratio, not as a probability of innocence. 2) Why is the 1-in-5 fibre only weak support? 3) What must you verify before relying on the timestamp? 4) Under what condition may you multiply the DNA and fibre likelihood ratios? 5) Who decides guilt after all this reasoning? Answers: 1) The match is about a million times more probable if the suspect is the source than if an unrelated person is. 2) Because roughly one coat in five matches by chance, a likelihood ratio of only about 5. 3) That the device clock was accurate and that independent records agree. 4) Only if the two findings are genuinely independent, and each is stated as weight rather than proof. 5) The court, not the scientist."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: converting the DNA random match probability into a ''chance of innocence'' - the prosecutor''s fallacy. Mistake: multiplying likelihood ratios for items that are not independent, or treating an unverified timestamp as established. Exit: (a) What is the combined likelihood ratio of the DNA and fibre if independent? (b) What should you do with the unverified timestamp? Answers: about 5,000,000 (1,000,000 x 5); set it aside until independent records corroborate it."}]}'::jsonb, 'When combining independent items, weight can build up, but only if the items are truly independent and each is stated as weight rather than proof - check both before multiplying anything.', 16),
  (16, 'Comprehensive final quiz', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"In the scene-to-court chain, the forensic scientist''s role is to...","options":["decide guilt","evaluate and report the weight of evidence","sentence the suspect","select the jury"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The scientist evaluates evidence; the court decides guilt - see ''What forensic science is''."},{"prompt":"A found trace can result from secondary transfer, which means...","options":["the trace was manufactured","material reached the surface via an intermediate, not by direct contact","the trace is always irrelevant","two people share DNA"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Secondary transfer routes material through an intermediate surface, complicating who touched what."},{"prompt":"Fingerprint comparisons are best described as...","options":["mechanical certainties","expert judgements that can err and need verification","based only on pattern class","unaffected by smudging"],"answer":1,"explanation":"They rely on judgement about minutiae and use blind verification to guard against error."},{"prompt":"At three independent loci with frequencies 1 in 10, 1 in 10, and 1 in 5, the random match probability is...","options":["1 in 25","1 in 500","1 in 125","1 in 50"],"answer":1,"explanation":"(1/10) x (1/10) x (1/5) = 1 in 500 by the product rule."},{"prompt":"A random match probability of 1 in a million means...","options":["a 1-in-a-million chance the suspect is innocent","an unrelated person has about a 1-in-a-million chance of matching by chance","the match is meaningless","guilt is certain"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It is the chance of a coincidental match by an unrelated person, not the probability of innocence."},{"prompt":"Which of these is closest to individualising evidence?","options":["a common blue cotton fibre","a glass refractive index shared by 1 in 100 fragments","a physical fit where two broken edges rejoin exactly","a common soil colour"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A physical fit is extraordinarily unlikely by chance, approaching a single-source conclusion."},{"prompt":"A presumptive drug test that gives the expected colour...","options":["identifies the exact substance","narrows possibilities but needs a confirmatory test","measures the dose","is more specific than a confirmatory test"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Presumptive tests suggest; confirmatory tests identify."},{"prompt":"A nearly round bloodstain on a smooth surface suggests a drop that...","options":["struck at a very shallow angle","fell roughly straight down","was wiped","belongs to two people"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Steep, near-vertical impact leaves a rounder stain; shallow impact elongates it."},{"prompt":"A likelihood ratio is the probability of the evidence under the prosecution account divided by...","options":["the number of suspects","its probability under the defence account","the prior odds","the cost of testing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It compares the evidence under two competing accounts."},{"prompt":"Announcing ''there is only a one-in-a-million chance the suspect is innocent'' from a 1-in-a-million match is...","options":["a correct summary","the prosecutor''s fallacy","a likelihood ratio","a hash value"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It swaps P(evidence | innocent) with P(innocent | evidence) and ignores the prior odds."},{"prompt":"In digital forensics, an editable timestamp should be treated as...","options":["an unquestionable fact","a claim to be checked against independent records","proof of who used the device","irrelevant in all cases"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Metadata can be edited and clocks can be wrong, so it needs corroboration."},{"prompt":"The main aim of blind verification and context management is to...","options":["speed up analysis","produce conclusions driven by the evidence and insulated from expectation","reduce laboratory costs","replace confirmatory tests"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Procedural safeguards keep irrelevant, biasing information from tilting a judgement."}]}'::jsonb, null, 12)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0004-0000-4000-8000-00000000002c' and l.position = v.pos
);
